// PARAM: --analysis sign
// The concrete result is fixed -- 0 - 5 is -5 and 0 < x is false -- and Sign
// still cannot say so.
//
// The mechanism is the domain's concretization, not its arithmetic. sign_sub
// answers Negative here, correctly. What loses it is the norm at the node's
// kind: gamma of Negative is every negative integer, with no bound, so the
// abstract value admits values far below int32's minimum whose wraparound is
// positive, and topping is the only sound answer. The bound exists in the
// program -- both operands were evaluated at int32 -- but the abstract value
// does not carry the kind it was computed at, so nothing at the norm can use
// it. A kind-tagged cell, whose concretization is intersected with the
// kind's range, is what makes Negative here mean [-2147483648, -1].
//
// 12-uint8_local_in_main.vimp under interval is the same program shape where
// the domain does carry the bound and the answer is exact.
void main() {
  int32 x;
  x := 0 - 5;
  __voblint_check(0 < x) // UNKNOWN
}
